From f8097abec40d532ab7ca28a0feca57bc854250b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: genspark_dev <genspark_dev@genspark.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 03:13:59 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 01/17] Give the referral its own attachments, and bring every
 file inside the seal
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An expert review of the attachment handling, in both directions, found four things.

The referral had an Attachments tab with nothing under it: the doctype carried no
attachment table, so the tab was permanently empty and a referral could not carry the
paperwork it hands over. It now has the same attachment table as the correspondence,
validated and shown on its panel.

The snapshot covered only the attachment table. Files linked to a record natively - an
upload through the File doctype or a gallery field - are not rows in that table, so a
file could be added to or replaced on a sealed record and every integrity check would
still pass. Read the linked files directly and fold them into the snapshot, so the seal
covers what the record can actually show.

The folder was a required free-text field, so it collected noise: the site holds a
literal "نن" typed by someone who had to fill it. It is optional now, with a description
and a controlled attachment type that defaults to Attachment.

The same file could be recorded twice under one type, which inflates the count and lets
two different snapshots describe the same paper. Refuse it.

Also take the newest File row when hashing a URL: a private and a public copy can share
one URL, and the first match was not necessarily the copy on the record.
