From 87072e11ce11303bfd834d3e12f526b39b68ff55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: genspark_dev <genspark_dev@genspark.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 04:18:41 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 07/17] Give the attachments a vocabulary, and let the
 framework keep the rest
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

What a file is, and where its paper original is filed, are the two things Frappe cannot express
about an attachment: there is no attachment type in the framework - it is an open feature
request from 2016 - and no notion of a physical archive location. Everything else was already
there, so this removes what duplicated it.

The attachment type was a Select field whose options could only change with a code change. It is
now a Link to Murasalat Attachment Type, seeded with five codes. The three codes already stored
on records are among them, so no data migration is needed. The folder field - free text, named
after a Frappe concept while the office read it as a shelf - became a Link to Murasalat Archive
Location, seeded with four places a small archive has. That name collision is what produced
"dfs" and "نن": everybody answered a different question.

Both tables store a short English code and display an Arabic name through title_field, and both
have allow_rename off, because renaming a code would orphan every record that stored it. Neither
ships permission rows: access is site configuration here, so a System Manager grants read from
Desk, and that step is written down rather than assumed.

Deleted: validate_attachment_rows, a duplicate check that compared file paths. Frappe already
refuses the same content twice on the same document in File.validate_duplicate_entry, by content
hash - stronger, and not ours to maintain. Storage, permissions, folders, change tracking and
deletion were never ours either: File.has_permission defers to the attached record, so a user who
cannot read a correspondence cannot read its attachments.

Three patches carry a site across, ordered by what each one needs. The column rename runs before
the schema sync, while the old column is still the only one of the two. The vocabulary runs after
it. The attachment index backfill runs last and out of numeric order - it writes rows whose type
is a Link, so the vocabulary has to exist first - and it was renumbered from v0_23, which had
never run: its old declaration aborted migrate with NameError before the module was imported, so
no Patch Log row existed and the number was free.

Also cleaned: the translation rows for the removed upload button and the old folder label are
gone, and both print formats map all five types.
