From: genspark_dev <genspark_dev@genspark.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 04:39:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 08/17] Make the site launchable in one command, and write the
 guide the clerk was missing

An expert review of launch readiness found the gap that no amount of code quality hides: the
application shipped no fixtures - correctly, since roles, permissions and Workflows are Desk
configuration - but it also had no single way to create that configuration, so a fresh install
needed six manual steps. The most dangerous was the Workflows. WORKFLOW_GOVERNANCE.md documents
what happens without them, and it is worse than an error: a transition with no task attached
still completes, writes nothing, and reports nothing. A Close transition without its task skips
the open-referral guard entirely and closes a file that still has live referrals.

setup/provision.py closes it. readiness prints a PASS/FAIL line per check and reads only;
apply refuses to run without confirm=True, is idempotent, and never overwrites an existing
Workflow, because an administrator may have edited it in Desk. It seeds the master data,
materialises the three roles and their permission rows, then creates the two Workflows with all
seven transitions, each carrying the lifecycle task it needs.

It reads the Workflow fieldnames from the live meta rather than hardcoding them. They are the
framework's fields, not ours, and a renamed one now raises a message naming what was looked for
and what the DocType offers - instead of half-building a Workflow. Asynchronous stays off on
every task row, since it runs the method outside the transition's transaction and turns every
lifecycle method into a silent no-op.

The second gap was the reader. Every document was written for an implementer: attachments,
print formats, seal integrity, workflows, verification. The person doing the work had none, so
docs/USER_GUIDE.md is written for them in Arabic - the roles and what each may not do, the
whole lifecycle as one diagram, each step with what it writes and what the system refuses, the
attachment rules, both print formats, the seven reports that matter, and a table of the common
refusals with what to do about them.

Also: the duplicate-row check deleted earlier was confirmed redundant - Frappe refuses the same
content twice on the same document by content hash - and both new master tables keep shipping no
permission rows, with the one-time grant they need written down instead of assumed.

Tests: 301, including a contract that every task the provisioning attaches is a name hooks.py
actually declares, that no transition can carry a task that does nothing, and that the guard in
apply runs before anything is written.
