From 08eba7a63abc1b49b94466be533a0c9407917397 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: genspark_dev <genspark_dev@genspark.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 04:59:23 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/17] Give management three reports that answer management's
 questions

Every report the app shipped indexed records: what is due, what is late, what is waiting for me.
None answered how the office performed over a period, or where the work was sitting across
departments. Those are the questions a general manager and a department head ask, and the data to
answer them was already in the records.

Murasalat Management Summary - office-wide totals per direction for a period: received,
registered, closed, sealed, open now, overdue and average days to close, with a total row and a
two-series chart. Its header summarises closed-of-received as a percentage.

Murasalat Department Workload - one row per organizational unit: what it holds now, the referrals
it owes, how many are overdue, what it completed in the period, the age of its oldest open file and
its average days to complete. Sorted worst-first, so the table is itself a worklist.

Murasalat Response Times - average days at each stage separately, because one cycle average cannot
locate a delay: a file that waited a week to be registered and one answered in a month produce the
same number and have different problems. The header names the slowest stage.

All three read through get_list(ignore_permissions=False), so a manager never sees a number built
from records they may not read, and all three carry period filters with native export and print.

One rule holds the arithmetic honest: work that is not finished is not counted as zero. A file that
has not been closed has no closing time, so days_between returns None and the average skips it -
counting it as zero would report a faster office the more work was left open.

No print format ships for the reports. Frappe 16 supports report print formats, but
PrintFormat.before_save forces custom_format=1 and standard="No" for them, so the framework itself
treats one as a site customisation rather than a document an app may ship. MANAGEMENT_REPORTS.md
gives the one-minute Desk steps for a site that wants an A4 layout.

Tests execute all three reports against stubbed rows. That is what caught the accumulator helper
carrying a record parameter it was called without - an undefined name in all three reports, which
reading the code had not shown.
