From a535c84ba5cc511597ab67fd5f077421ccbe7acc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: genspark_dev <genspark_dev@genspark.ai>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 01:49:07 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/17] feat(notifications): let Frappe tell the right person,
 on its own mechanism

Five questions a user has to be able to answer, and the native surface that answers
each - with no notification engine, no inbox, no scheduler and no second assignment
model in this app.

Assignment Rule on Murasalat Referral (Based on Field -> recipient_user) is what was
missing: frappe.desk.form.assign_to.add already creates the ToDo, shares the record
when the assignee cannot read it, and writes the framework's own "Assignment"
Notification Log, so a second "a referral arrived" notification would be the duplicate
the brief forbids. It is wired to doc_events["*"]["on_update"], and apply() calls
apply_assign only when nothing is assigned, so an unchanged referral cannot announce
itself twice. Its close condition on Completed/Cancelled is what ends both the
assignment and the reminders.

Two native Notifications carry the dates: Days Before / Days After on due_date, one day
each, run by the framework's own trigger_daily_alerts. Recipients are the assignees
(send_to_all_assignees), resolved from the open ToDo rows at send time, so responsibility
that ended takes its reminders with it. The duplicate guard is a condition, not code: it
refuses when a Notification Log of that kind already exists for the document, using the
frappe namespace the framework puts in a condition. The payload is the referral and its
date only - nothing from the parent correspondence - because nothing checks that a
recipient can read the record before a log is written for them.

The framework's own assignment notice becomes Arabic through translations/ar.csv, which
is how a native message needs no code at all. Nothing was added for workflow actions:
the native Workflow Action list already shows pending transitions, and send_email_alert
would email every holder of the role, which the brief rules out.

provision.apply creates all three rows and readiness reports each as PASS/FAIL.
