Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 02:43:27 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] fix(referrals): reconcile a migrated record with the
 vocabulary and the state it shows
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two symptoms a real site produced, one root cause: rows converted from the old child
table kept what that table could hold, and nothing re-validated them or read them the
way the application now writes them.

Receiving a referral was refused with "Recipient Type must be User or Department."
The record's recipient type was "Organization" - the value this Select offered before it
was renamed to Department (both refusal messages are still in ar.csv, which is how the
rename is visible). v0_21 copied the child row's values verbatim, and nothing validates a
migrated row until a lifecycle action runs the DocType's invariants, so the refusal
surfaced at the first Receive. A patch now brings those rows into the declared vocabulary,
deriving the target from the link the record actually carries before consulting the old
name - a row naming a user and no department is a user referral whatever its type field
once said. Rows that cannot be derived are reported, not guessed, and the refusal now
prints the value it refused, because an empty field and an older vocabulary otherwise
read the same.

The second symptom was the same record disagreeing with itself: the state badge read
"Sent" while the panel beside it read "لم تُرسل". The panel decided sentness from the
`sent_on` timestamp alone - which the child table had no way to carry - so a migrated
referral never counted as sent. Sentness, completion and cancellation are now read from
the state as well as the stamp, in the list KPIs, the decorated rows and the document
panel; the correspondence panel reads its seal the same way. Cancelled work also leaves
the open set, which is the rule the notification close condition and the cancellation
transition already follow.

And the reason a *new* referral could be sent with no `sent_on` at all is now closed:
`_ensure_workflow` never rewrites a Workflow that exists - deliberately, since an
administrator may have edited it - but it never completed its transition tasks either, so
a Workflow created before that feature, or by hand in Desk, kept transitions that change
the state and run nothing, without a word. `apply` now tops up only the missing task rows
and leaves every existing one untouched, and `readiness` reports a Workflow whose
transitions run no method as its own FAIL line.
